WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.310
SPEAKER: The 2021 State
of DevOps Report released

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:07.690
last year--

00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:10.330
it contained findings
about how teams navigate

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:13.300
key areas of software delivery.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:16.000
We talked to two of the
researchers, Dave Stanke

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.780
and Nathen Harvey, about
the report's section on SRE.

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:23.410
Were there any surprising
findings in the report?

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:26.650
DAVE STANKE: I was surprised
to see how widespread SRE

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:28.090
practices are.

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:30.580
It's often been believed
that this is something that's

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:33.520
limited to the Googles
of the world or Facebook,

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:36.160
with their production
engineering discipline, which

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:37.960
is similar to SRE.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:42.100
What we found is that 52% of
the organizations we studied

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:45.130
are practicing SRE
techniques to some extent.

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:47.920
At the same time, there's
a lot of room for growth.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:51.310
Among our elite respondents,
only 10% of them

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:54.250
are practicing every reliability
engineering technique

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:55.150
at its fullest.

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:58.227
So everyone has room to grow.

00:00:58.227 --> 00:01:00.310
SPEAKER: What did the site
reliability engineering

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:02.953
section of the report focus on?

00:01:02.953 --> 00:01:04.870
DAVE STANKE: So this
year, for the first time,

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:08.260
DORA studied Site Reliability
Engineering, or SRE,

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:11.740
and we looked at what practices
are being done among operations

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:13.660
teams in our study and
what kind of outcomes

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:15.430
they're achieving as a result.

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:18.880
NATHEN HARVEY: SRE is Site
Reliability Engineering.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.670
In my mind, it always starts
with that word, reliability.

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:24.153
What does it mean
to be reliable?

00:01:24.153 --> 00:01:25.570
At the end of the
day, reliability

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:30.130
is about whether or not your
customers can trust the service

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:32.110
that they're getting
from your applications

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:35.090
or from the services
that you're building.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:37.300
SPEAKER: What did you
find separates elite teams

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:39.580
from the rest?

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:41.590
DAVE STANKE: So
the best teams are

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:45.220
the ones that are really
treating reliability

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:48.970
as a product feature, as an
aspect of their experience

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:50.980
of what they're
delivering to the user,

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:53.260
and prioritizing it accordingly.

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:56.590
We find that it really helps
to define reliability in terms

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:57.880
of user-facing behavior.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:00.760
We don't want to look at
how do our machines feel--

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.250
do their-- processors
overloaded, are there-- is

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:04.000
there memory fold?

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:05.740
That may be important
operational data.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:08.110
But it doesn't define
whether our system

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:09.800
is reliable for our users.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:13.750
So with SRE-- and what
the highest performers do

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:16.000
is they look at it
to say, is our system

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.250
delivering on behalf of
what the user is expecting?

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:21.130
Is it serving the user need?

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:24.340
And then we use frameworks,
like the SLO framework,

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:28.300
to operationalize that so that
we can measure in real time

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:31.270
the degree to which we are
keeping up with our promises

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:32.870
to the user.

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:36.640
SPEAKER: How can teams start
implementing these practices?

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:38.140
NATHEN HARVEY: The
best way to apply

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:39.880
these findings is
to first figure out

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:41.260
where are you as a team.

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:42.940
So get your team together.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:45.070
Get the people that are
responsible for building

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:47.170
the application, for
running the application.

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:48.880
Bring them together in a room.

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:52.240
And together, take the
DORA DevOps quick check.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:56.140
You'll find that at
cloud.google.com/devops.

00:02:56.140 --> 00:03:00.100
With this quick check, you
answer five quick questions

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:03.610
and really help assess how
is my team doing today.

00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:05.590
With this information
in hand, you

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:08.290
can then start to look at the
capabilities that are going

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:09.870
to improve that performance.

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:11.620
And of course, we'll
provide some guidance

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:14.050
on what those
capabilities are and where

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:18.180
you should get started
next improving your team.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:20.680
SPEAKER: What would you say is
the core idea of this section

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:22.732
of the report?

00:03:22.732 --> 00:03:24.190
NATHEN HARVEY: The
biggest takeaway

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:28.810
is that you don't have to
trade off speed for stability.

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:32.090
Speed and stability work
hand-in-hand together.

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:34.450
So the most important
thing that you can do

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:36.370
is think about the
users of your service

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:39.220
and make sure that you're
delivering the right value

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:42.450
in a reliable way to them.

